WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.580
The first film separated living interactions from nonliving conditions.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.820
Can one nonliving condition predict where oysters might survive?

3
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.180
Return to the same Virginia landscape.

4
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.880
Follow salt from the upper James River to the mouth of the Bay.

5
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:24.590
Salinity is the amount of salt dissolved in water.

6
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.110
The upper James is fresh, while ocean water is salty.

7
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.090
In the Chesapeake estuary, river water and ocean water mix.

8
00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.670
Salinity usually rises toward the Bay mouth, but the mixing zone can move.

9
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.730
A sampling boat starts at the upper tidal James.

10
00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.670
At each station, a probe measures the water below the surface.

11
00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.530
Each stop adds a point to the same river path.

12
00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:56.610
Watch the points climb as the boat moves toward the Bay.

13
00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.930
Now place an oyster beside the boat.

14
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:05.510
Where along this fresh-to-salty path should oysters first find suitable water?

15
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.090
Make a prediction before the boat moves again.

16
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:19.607
The oyster first fits the brackish lower river, not the fresh upper river.

17
00:01:21.207 --> 00:01:25.427
Eastern oysters can die when water stays very low in salt for a long time.

18
00:01:26.827 --> 00:01:30.527
Wild oyster reefs grow in the lower James, where the water is mixed.

19
00:01:31.617 --> 00:01:34.757
The measured station means make the gradient visible.

20
00:01:36.197 --> 00:01:39.897
Low values sit upstream, and higher values sit near the Bay mouth.

21
00:01:41.157 --> 00:01:45.677
The difference between two stations must be calculated, not guessed from spacing.

22
00:01:47.077 --> 00:01:51.817
The lesson table lets you subtract exact values and test the oyster range.

23
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.260
Range matches are not sharp borders.

24
00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.000
Widgeon grass can fit every station listed.

25
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.480
The oyster's range overlaps the eelgrass stations near the Bay mouth.

26
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.980
The crews measured water; they did not count organisms at those stations.

27
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.180
Suitable salinity suggests habitat; it does not prove an organism was there.

28
00:02:17.233 --> 00:02:19.833
Salinity also shifts with rain and season.

29
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:24.253
The boundary slides as fresh and salt water keep mixing.

30
00:02:25.553 --> 00:02:29.673
A station's value can change even though the river and Bay remain in place.

31
00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:34.253
One summer sample is a snapshot, not a permanent border.

32
00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:39.367
Salinity is powerful evidence, but it is only one condition.

33
00:02:40.867 --> 00:02:44.787
Light, temperature, water movement, predators, and food can matter too.

34
00:02:46.207 --> 00:02:50.847
Living interactions and nonliving conditions help decide what can live where.

35
00:02:52.047 --> 00:02:56.707
The strongest explanation connects the pattern to both sides of the ecosystem.

36
00:02:58.187 --> 00:03:01.047
Use the station data to test that rule, with limits.
